WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:06.080
Narrator: We have witnessed not one

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.960
but two monumental battles
in the final season

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.290
of "Game of Thrones."

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.850
The King's Landing episode was
polarizing to say the least,

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:17.260
just as the Winterfell Battle
was two episodes before it,

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:20.420
but one of these clashes was
an emotional roller coaster

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.030
with an intensely satisfying conclusion,

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:25.730
while the other just felt anticlimactic.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.960
The show may have made some
questionable lighting decisions

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.750
in "The Long Night," but
director Miguel Sapochnik

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.080
managed to hold our
white-knuckled attention

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.660
throughout the hour-plus battle

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.980
by giving us essentially
four movies in one.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.940
Here's why the Battle of Winterfell

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.950
outshined the Battle of King's Landing,

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.950
and, in fact, why it will go down

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.800
as one of the best battles in TV history.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:48.720
Be warned:

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.983
Spoilers are coming.

00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:54.250
Like "The Long Night,"
"The Bells" was directed

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.800
by Miguel Sapochnik, the mastermind

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.430
behind seminal battle
episodes like "Hardhome"

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:01.410
and "Battle of the Bastards."

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:04.550
But this most recent entry
felt more like a massacre

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:05.383
than a battle.

00:01:05.383 --> 00:01:08.310
The show spent seasons
building up to this attack,

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.830
yet all it takes is a few "dracarys"

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.920
to take the Westerosi capital.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.660
After that, the episode
devolves into a spectacle

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.670
of mass slaughter with most
of the victims nameless.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.060
When it comes to extended war sequences,

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.800
monotony is really the enemy.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.060
In contrast, in the Winterfell episode,

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.600
Sapochnik wisely staved off monotony

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.240
by dividing the fight
into four different acts,

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.880
each fitting into a
different cinematic genre.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.740
Pace changes were key
to avoid battle fatigue.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.170
Looking at the time the
characters spent on-screen

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.010
over the duration of the episode,

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.600
you can see how act
one is mostly comprised

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.560
of short, jittery snippets
to get us on edge.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.720
That suspense turns into
a protracted horror show

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.810
with Arya's stealth scene in act two,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.560
filmed in extended takes with
a few memorable jump scares.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.240
This is followed by slightly more

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.880
quick-cutting action in act three,

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.630
and finally, a slow-motion
drama in act four.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.050
In the Battle of King's Landing,
arguably the tensest moment

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:11.220
is when silence falls on the capital

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.100
and the bells ring to signal capitulation.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.300
After Daenerys ignores the surrender

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.600
and unleashes her firepower on the city,

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.410
the question becomes not
whether Cersei will fall

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:22.243
but when.

00:02:22.243 --> 00:02:25.320
Suddenly, the stakes just don't
seem all that high anymore.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.960
Meanwhile, the entire first
act of the Battle of Winterfell

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.990
is a masterclass in tension.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.000
There's a long, eerie silence

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.330
punctuated by spare bits of dialog

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:38.400
with just over 10
comprehensible lines spoken

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.660
within the first 20
minutes of the episode.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.790
The charging Dothraki look
like darts or arrows of light

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.700
advancing into the blackness.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.000
When their war cries suddenly go silent,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.400
that's when the real darkness sets in.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.040
It's not only the
annihilation of the Dothraki,

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.900
who have been with us from the
very beginning of the show,

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.260
but also the extinguishing of
that initial glimmer of hope.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.290
More terrifying is, as
Sapochnik described it,

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.640
"Like any good monster
movie, we're not allowed

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.120
to see the monsters."

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.330
This is the same technique famously used

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:12.260
by Steven Spielberg in "Jaws,"

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:15.630
drawing on the Hitchcockian
idea of the unseen enemy.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.850
Throughout this episode,
we'll see that the setting

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:21.280
of each act is in service
of its specific genre.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.070
Here, we're on open fields
in a pitch-black night,

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.660
where, for a long time,
it's impossible to see

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:27.870
what we're up against.

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.470
Even as the wights
charge, the initial stages

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.380
of hand-to-hand combat are dominated

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.420
by a sense of total confusion,

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.690
especially after the ice storm hits.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:38.800
In terms of battle clarity, well,

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:42.070
that's one domain where
King's Landing clearly wins.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:43.640
It's shot in broad daylight,

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.740
where we can see every
detail of the bloodbath.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.240
"Long Night," on the other hand,

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.230
was set in the darkest hours

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.860
in a controversial
decision heavily criticized

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.320
by the show's fans.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.840
It meant that we have
to rely on silhouettes

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:00.260
to discern the action or at
least to get the gist of it,

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.290
but that's very much the
point in this battle,

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:04.030
especially during this act.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.130
It not only instills in us
that fear of the unknowable,

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:09.340
it's also an articulation
of how this fight

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.120
would really feel on the ground:

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.787
frenzied, chaotic, and
totally disorienting.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:22.530
As the living are forced to
retreat back into the castle,

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:25.340
the chaos quickly devolves
into a horror show

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:27.610
that feels something
like a zombie invasion.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.010
It's turning to hell for every character.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:32.640
We can now see in horrifying clarity

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.830
how the wights move in that
contortionist, glitchy way

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:37.730
reminiscent of the kinds of monsters

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:39.700
seen in movies like "The Grudge."

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.860
Full slasher mode soon gives way

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:44.150
to a quieter type of survival horror.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.910
As we move inside the
buildings of Winterfell,

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.610
Arya's cat-and-mouse
sequence feels like a tribute

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:52.470
to the iconic kitchen
hunt from "Jurassic Park."

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:53.870
Both of these scenes take place

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.680
in familiar settings that
are supposed to be safe

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.800
but are actually crawling with invaders.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.480
The familiarity of Winterfell's interiors

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:05.820
is an especially terrifying
aspect of this scene.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.950
Until now, the castle's inside spaces

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:11.140
have offered refuge from
the chaos raging outside.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.900
Now, as showrunner David Benioff said,

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.990
"These hallways and rooms where
we spent a lot of good times

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.330
and quiet times with
characters we care about

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:21.520
have become a horror set."

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.290
The threat is more intimate now,

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:25.220
and the deathly silence makes moments

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.050
like this inverted jump scare

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:29.290
truly jarring.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:31.360
Our sentimental ties to Winterfell

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.920
make this a more ghastly scenario.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.670
As viewers, we never had that
kind of relationship with,

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:38.430
for example, King's Landing.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.440
That city's associated with the suffering

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.650
of many characters at the
cruel hands of the Lannisters,

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:45.810
so Daenerys' onslaught doesn't seem

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:48.080
quite so at odds with the setting.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.340
On the other hand, the sight of Winterfell

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:51.850
overrun with the dead

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:55.160
feels like a terrifying
break with the past.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.410
Battle drums lead us into the next act,

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:01.243
and we're plunged into
a full-on action movie.

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:05.120
We follow Jon as he
charges through the castle,

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:06.950
heading right into the thick of battle.

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:08.950
Here, Sapochnik takes another page

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:10.450
out of Steven Spielberg's book,

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:12.430
using the battlefield-camera approach

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:15.300
that worked so well in
"Saving Private Ryan."

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.230
The camera flows through the castle,

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:19.350
staying with Jon every step of the way.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.850
He's running full-tilt and so are we,

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:25.260
weaving through scene after
scene of complete devastation,

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:28.350
what Sapochnik called
the "it's f-----" shots.

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:30.800
The camera ducks underneath
swords and daggers

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.870
and dodges spinning
bodies and jumping wights.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.800
We're inserted into the action,

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.930
and we can feel how all-encompassing

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.740
and claustrophobic it is.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.030
As the trench is dying down, the moonlight

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:44.040
suddenly becomes introduced again.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.870
We're starting to come full circle.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:46.810
The end is near,

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:49.870
and as we haven't seen Arya
for a good chunk of time,

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:52.470
we're tricked into putting
all our hope into Jon.

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:54.170
When his prospects look grim,

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:57.360
that's when the dismay
really starts to set in.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.500
And then, we get to the
pure drama of act four.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:03.010
The Night King is advancing
into the godswood,

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:05.230
the most sacred place in Winterfell.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:07.550
The ancient weirwood where Bran awaits him

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:10.880
has stood at the center of
that grove for 10,000 years,

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.550
and it's been the site
of many fond memories.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:16.730
The Night King's invasion into
this touchstone of Winterfell

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:19.240
feels tragic and nightmarish.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:22.210
Consistent with these
extraordinary circumstances,

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.790
the sequence pretty much breaks

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:25.950
all the stylistic rules of the show.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:27.620
It's in extreme slow motion,

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:30.260
mostly shot in 96 frames a second.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:33.010
Slow-mo is a style that
"Game of Thrones" reserves

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:36.040
for only its most dramatic
and tragic moments.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:38.490
Instinctually, we know what this means.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:40.080
All bets are off.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.780
This heightened reality is
reminiscent of Akira Kurosawa,

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.760
the Japanese filmmaker who pioneered

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:48.380
the use of slow motion,
often to heighten the drama

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:50.270
in a climatic scene.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.020
Sapochnik is using slow motion

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:55.590
in the same haunting,
anguished way as Kurosawa,

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:58.020
whom he's cited as an inspiration before.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.240
This is also by far the most screen time

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.700
the Night King's ever gotten,

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:03.980
more than double the
amount he got in "Hardhome"

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.340
or "Beyond the Wall."

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:09.200
It seems unreal seeing so
much of him and so close up.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.350
As Sapochnik said, the whole thing

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:14.370
is designed to feel like
a surreal nightmare.

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:16.680
Dany's reign of terror two episodes later

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.870
would've benefited from
some of these close-ups.

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.530
After all, the Dragon
Queen's total heel turn

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:25.260
transformed her suddenly
into a rogue tyrant.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:28.160
This moral reversal
might've been more chilling

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:32.250
if we'd seen the reaction
or nonreaction on her face

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.830
as she incinerated the city below her.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:36.960
Instead, we're detached
from this new villain

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.130
after she embarks on her rampage.

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:42.880
She and Drogon just become
distant figures in the sky.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:45.910
The nightmare at "The Long
Night" is much more immersive.

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:47.500
Ambient noise is muted.

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:50.640
The sound of peoples' grunts
and blows become muffled,

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.070
and it feels like a trance state.

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:55.130
The mood of despair is reinforced

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:58.780
by the Night King's theme,
a haunting piano-led track.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:09.950
And then comes Arya, delivering
what The New Yorker called

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:12.020
"The Stab Heard Round the World."

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:15.350
Most of the action up till
now has been hard to follow.

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:17.360
For an hour plus, we've been in a state

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:19.110
of total disorientation.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:22.120
Whether it was a result of
the shaky handheld camera

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:24.210
and quick editing of the first act

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:27.130
or the hyper-surreal
nature of this final act,

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:30.210
they kept us in the dark, both
literally and figuratively,

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:33.460
for so long, but Arya
delivering the fatal blow

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:35.730
brings a moment of sudden clarity,

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.090
slicing through our dreamlike state.

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:40.500
See how the storyboards from this sequence

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:43.330
emphasize the clarity of every movement.

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:45.650
This was an immediately iconic moment

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:47.830
and a dramatically satisfying conclusion

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:51.690
to a battle that almost
ended in existential ruin.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:53.780
If we graph the main narrative beats

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:55.540
over the duration of the episode,

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:56.373
the progression of the battle

00:09:56.373 --> 00:09:57.206
would look something like this.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.280
What makes "The Long Night" special?

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:03.020
Well, the structure of the plot

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:05.230
matches the shape of the battle itself.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:07.310
As the dead advance on Winterfell,

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:09.410
with each retreat of the living,

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:12.110
the invading threat
penetrates deeper and deeper

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:13.920
into the heart of Winterfell.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:17.710
As each line of defense falls,
the intensity heightens.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:20.640
The narrative pattern of concentric rings

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:24.070
is fitting for a show that
loves to loop back in time,

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:26.090
whether with explicit flashbacks

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:28.573
or more subtle visual parallels.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:32.670
Luwin: Is there going to be
a battle in the godswood?

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:34.160
Narrator: Throughout the
battle of Winterfell,

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:37.200
they kept us invested by
keeping the focus on character

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.530
with references to memorable dialog

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:42.430
and formative events from earlier seasons.

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:44.230
Moments like these reminded us

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:47.670
of our longtime personal
investment in these characters.

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.240
The episode was firmly rooted in the past,

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:54.170
and as a result, the emotional
stakes felt quite high.

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:56.960
Comparing the battles of
Winterfell and King's Landing

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.640
shows that it takes more
than just massive scale

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:03.680
and spectacular visuals to
make a great war sequence.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:06.720
"The Long Night" was a
brilliantly torturous ride

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.570
in which we experienced
the full spectrum of war,

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:11.920
the uncertainty and doubt giving way

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:14.930
into terror, adrenaline, and tragedy.

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:17.930
I'm not afraid to call it a masterpiece.

